WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.676
Containers make leak prevention more
interesting because they separate storage from

2
00:00:04.756 --> 00:00:05.327
ownership.

3
00:00:05.407 --> 00:00:08.782
A container may own its elements and
release them during destruction,

4
00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:11.265
or it may only reference objects owned
elsewhere.

5
00:00:11.345 --> 00:00:12.717
Both designs can be correct,

6
00:00:12.797 --> 00:00:16.000
but the choice must be documented before
callers insert pointers.

7
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.341
Arrays of pointers require clear rules for
releasing each element.

8
00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:23.539
Freeing the array itself does not release
each pointed-to allocation unless the code

9
00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:24.591
explicitly does so.

10
00:00:24.671 --> 00:00:27.017
If some elements are owned and some are
borrowed,

11
00:00:27.097 --> 00:00:28.927
cleanup becomes hard to reason about.

12
00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:31.981
A consistent ownership policy is usually
easier to maintain.

13
00:00:32.061 --> 00:00:35.836
Linked structures and trees often need
recursive or iterative cleanup logic.

14
00:00:35.916 --> 00:00:38.891
Each node may own child nodes, strings,
buffers, or metadata.

15
00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:42.975
Cleanup should visit every owned
allocation exactly according to the structure's

16
00:00:43.055 --> 00:00:43.913
ownership model.

17
00:00:43.993 --> 00:00:45.251
For very deep structures,

18
00:00:45.331 --> 00:00:48.591
iterative cleanup may be preferable to
avoid excessive call depth.

19
00:00:48.671 --> 00:00:52.618
Structs with nested allocations need
cleanup functions that release every owned

20
00:00:52.698 --> 00:00:53.041
field.

21
00:00:53.121 --> 00:00:56.038
A structure containing several allocated
strings, arrays,

22
00:00:56.118 --> 00:01:00.008
or child objects should have a single
cleanup routine that knows which fields are

23
00:01:00.088 --> 00:01:01.461
owned and how to reset state.

24
00:01:01.541 --> 00:01:04.343
Callers should not need to remember every
internal field.

25
00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:08.427
Duplicated strings need the same ownership
discipline as other heap allocations.

26
00:01:08.507 --> 00:01:11.253
A copied string returned from a helper,
stored in a table,

27
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:14.136
or assigned to a structure field should
have a known owner.

28
00:01:14.216 --> 00:01:18.277
Replacing a duplicated string usually
means releasing the old value before storing

29
00:01:18.357 --> 00:01:21.102
the new one, unless ownership is
transferred elsewhere.

30
00:01:21.182 --> 00:01:25.129
Removing an item from a container should
define whether removal also releases the

31
00:01:25.209 --> 00:01:25.495
item.

32
00:01:25.575 --> 00:01:29.179
A remove operation might return ownership
to the caller, destroy the item,

33
00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:31.032
or only detach a borrowed reference.

34
00:01:31.112 --> 00:01:34.887
The function name and documentation should
make that behavior clear enough to

35
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:36.740
prevent leaks and repeated cleanup.
